WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.519
Hi, I'm Twinkle!

00:00:02.519 --> 00:00:07.846
Muse Glimmer is Meta's thirty-billion-parameter agent model, built for one consumer graphics card.

00:00:07.846 --> 00:00:09.485
Here's how it fits.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:13.275
The idea: design backwards from memory.

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:17.542
The weights, a long-context cache, a vision encoder and a drafter all

00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:20.386
have to fit in twenty-four gigabytes at once.

00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:26.897
Four-bit quantization shrinks the language model to sixteen point seven six gigabytes.

00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:31.037
Beside it: a vision encoder, a speculative drafter, and the KV

00:00:31.037 --> 00:00:32.390
cache at full context.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.132
Together that's twenty-one point six gigabytes, with two point four to spare.

00:00:37.132 --> 00:00:38.572
The claim is arithmetic.

00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:42.846
Three of every four layers are local.

00:00:42.846 --> 00:00:47.287
Each token looks back two thousand and forty-eight tokens, so their cache stops growing.

00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:52.581
Every fourth layer is global and sees everything, with no positional encoding.

00:00:52.581 --> 00:00:55.830
The argument: then longer contexts hold no surprises.

00:00:57.192 --> 00:01:00.662
Make all fifty-two layers global, and the cache grows to six

00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:01.924
point nine eight gigabytes.

00:01:01.924 --> 00:01:03.186
It no longer fits.

00:01:03.606 --> 00:01:07.120
Three local per global, plus sixteen-to-one grouped queries, keep it at

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.398
one point eight three.

00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:14.003
A five-layer drafter proposes sixteen tokens in one forward pass.

00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:16.792
The main model verifies them in parallel.

00:01:17.354 --> 00:01:19.861
Decoding reads every weight for each token.

00:01:19.861 --> 00:01:22.369
Verifying sixteen at once shares that read.

00:01:22.789 --> 00:01:26.725
So the gain tracks spare compute: three point one times on an

00:01:26.725 --> 00:01:29.678
RTX fifty-ninety, one point five on an M4 Max.

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:35.414
Sliding windows, grouped queries and four-bit weights aren't three optimizations.

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:37.550
They're one memory budget, allocated.

00:01:39.192 --> 00:01:43.362
To recap: a budget that closes, sliding windows keeping the cache small,

00:01:43.362 --> 00:01:45.447
and a drafter spending spare compute.

00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:47.996
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.996 --> 00:01:48.605
I'm Twinkle.

00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:48.909
Bye!
